            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fraudō, fraudāre — \"to cheat, to defraud\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Caupō malus hospitēs fraudat. The bad innkeeper cheats his guests."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fraudō, fraudāre, fraudāvī, fraudātum, with the person in the accusative and the thing in the ablative. English defraud comes from dēfraudāre."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fraudō is built on fraus, fraudis 'harm, deceit' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English fraud comes from the noun and defraud from the compound dēfraudāre."
            }
          ]
        },
